    <title>Case 02: Telugu and Kannada Under Constraint | Cinekorn | Jalak Patel</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Honest packaging for weak source material. Telugu from zero activity, Kannada rebuilt with tenure vs legacy views separated.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://jalak-patel-190.github.io/jalak-portfolio/case-studies/cinekorn/02-telugu-kannada.html">
    <meta property="og:title" content="Making Weak Source Material Work | Cinekorn | Jalak Patel">
    <meta property="og:description" content="Telugu 3,516 views from 31 tenure videos. Kannada 7,425 attributable views, not the full 78,782 lifetime total.">
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://jalak-patel-190.github.io/jalak-portfolio/media/cinekorn/yt-movies.png">
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="../../favicon.png">
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bebas+Neue&family=Space+Grotesk:wght@400;500;600;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="cinekorn-case-shared.css">
</head>
<body>
    <a class="skip-link" href="#context">Skip to content</a>
    <header>
        <div class="header-inner">
            <a href="../../index.html" class="logo-wrapper">
                <span class="logo">JALAK PATEL</span>
                <span class="logo-subtitle">Creative Strategist</span>
            </a>
            <button class="nav-toggle" id="navToggle" aria-label="Toggle navigation" aria-expanded="false">Menu</button>
            <nav id="mainNav">
                <a href="index.html">Cinekorn Hub</a>
                <a href="../../index.html#work">All Cases</a>
                <a href="../../index.html#contact" class="nav-cta">Get In Touch</a>
            </nav>
        </div>
    </header>

    <main class="container">
        <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
            <a href="../../index.html">Home</a> · <a href="index.html">Cinekorn</a> · Case 02
        </nav>

        <header class="case-hero">
            <p class="eyebrow">Case 02 · Cinekorn Entertainment</p>
            <h1>Making Weak Source Material Work</h1>
            <p class="lede">Not every library arrives with ideal prints. Telugu and Kannada required honest packaging, not fake premium polish.</p>
            <div class="metric-row">
                <div class="metric-pill">Telugu 3,516 <span>tenure views</span></div>
                <div class="metric-pill">Kannada 7,425 <span>attributable</span></div>
                <div class="metric-pill">31 + 98 <span>videos</span></div>
            </div>
        </header>

        <section class="section" id="context">
            <p class="section-label">Context</p>
            <h2>Limited catalogues, older prints</h2>
            <p>Cinekorn Telugu and Cinekorn Kannada included older films, lower-resolution prints, and relatively small active libraries. Material existed, but it could not always compete with newer, higher-quality digital releases.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="problem">
            <p class="section-label">Problem</p>
            <h2>You cannot upscale honesty</h2>
            <p>A low-resolution film cannot be made high-resolution through better packaging. Presenting older material as if it were new sets the wrong expectation.</p>
            <p>The real question was not only "How do we get more views?" It was: <strong>How do we make available content relevant to someone who may still want to watch it?</strong></p>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="strategy">
            <p class="section-label">Strategy</p>
            <h2>Position the age of the catalogue</h2>
            <p>Titles and descriptions used terms such as <strong>Old Movies</strong>, <strong>Classic Cinema</strong>, and <strong>Cult Classic</strong> so viewers understood what they would see before clicking.</p>
            <p>Thumbnails followed the same principle. Where source quality was too poor for a meaningful custom thumbnail, I did not force a heavily designed frame simply to look polished. Packaging had to match the material.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="execution">
            <p class="section-label">Execution and evidence</p>
            <h2>Two channels, two attribution stories</h2>

            <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:1.25rem 0 0.5rem;">Cinekorn Telugu</h3>
            <p>No recorded activity before 2026 in the supplied data. Lifetime views: <strong>3,516</strong> from <strong>31 videos</strong> published after takeover. Attribution is relatively clear. This is tenure activity, not historical channel performance.</p>

            <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:1.25rem 0 0.5rem;">Cinekorn Kannada</h3>
            <p>Lifetime views: <strong>78,782</strong>. That total is not the active library alone.</p>

            <div class="bars" id="kannadaBars">
                <div class="bar-row">
                    <div class="bar-label"><span>Pre-2026 content (no longer in library)</span><span>71,357</span></div>
                    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="--w:90%" data-bar></div></div>
                </div>
                <div class="bar-row">
                    <div class="bar-label"><span>Attributable to my period (98 videos)</span><span>7,425</span></div>
                    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="--w:9.5%" data-bar></div></div>
                </div>
                <p class="bars-caption">Share of 78,782 lifetime views: legacy vs tenure attributable.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="callout">
                <strong>Important:</strong> Using 78,782 as if it represented my work would give the wrong picture. The honest number for the period is <strong>7,425 views across 98 videos</strong>.
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="results">
            <p class="section-label">Results</p>
            <h2>Practical, not magical</h2>
            <p>This was not a case where weak material suddenly became premium. Channels continued publishing despite catalogue limits. Packaging reflected what the material was rather than hiding its limits.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="takeaway">
            <p class="section-label">Takeaway</p>
            <h2>What this showed</h2>
            <p>Content strategy is not always about creating something bigger. Sometimes the job is to understand the material honestly, identify the audience for it, and package it so that audience has a reason to click, especially when content is limited.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="whats-next">
            <h2>What is next</h2>
            <div class="next-grid">
                <a class="next-card" href="01-data-tooling.html">
